I use Gravity for reading, posting and some months of archive (depending 
on group, less for high-traffic rec.games.pinball, more for low-traffic 
rec.game.roguelike.nethack). Gravity does an enormous amount of things 
that Google can't do, as hinted at in another post in this thread.

There are a number of things that I find really annoying with Google 
Groups' interface, and even more with GGB. This is for reading posts 
only, I have never tried posting from Google so I can't say much about 
that. And I never use the binaries groups at all, so I can't say much 
about such things either.


I still use Google Groups for whenever I want to make searches for 
longer back than I have archived myself. Gravity integrates nicely with 
Google, so that if I click on a message-ID in a post, it can take me to 
that message in Google Groups.

I don't think I would be using newsgroups more than for reference 
searches if GGB was my only way to access. It is too 'clumsy' a tool for 
me, when I know what I want a newsreader to do, it would feel painful to 
resort to it.

Since you bring up the threading capabilities of Google, I must say that 
they seem at best like a very crude mimicry of the threading 
functionality in Gravity and other 'proper' newsreaders I've used.

To sum up - I think Google Groups is the best tool available for some 
things, and extremely bad at other things. So I use it for the things I 
like it for, and use other tools for other things.

Obviously, if you are happy with Google Groups you are happy with Google 
Groups. It is your choice and I am not writing to bash it, just to 
explain my choice and perhaps get a better understanding of yours.

